---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import data from "../data/controlbun.json";

// The site had three outbound links before this page: two to Hugging Face's own
// sign-in pages and one to the bytes of the single pinned artifact. So a reader
// who finished the argument on `/about/` and had a response to it had nowhere to
// put it, and a reader who wanted to check a claim could not find the code.
// `V1.md` B3 was closed when `/about/` said who built it, which answered "whose
// is it" and left "how do I reply" open.
//
// **Two links and a paragraph.** Nothing here receives: a `mailto:` is an
// anchor, the browser hands it to whatever the reader uses for mail, and this
// page is gone. Same criterion as `/sign-in/`, which is the other page whose
// whole job is to point somewhere else.
//
// **Mail is still not a submission route, and this page said otherwise for a
// day.** It was edited on 2026-09-20 to say mail takes a submission record,
// which was true of the hour between `/signed-in/` producing a downloadable
// record and the table existing for it to be sent to. The sentence here has
// always been "the day that changes it will be written down"; the day changed,
// what it changed to is a route on `/signed-in/`, and `DECISIONS.md` 2026-09-20
// is where it is written down.
//
// **The address is written out, not obfuscated, and that is a decision rather
// than an oversight.** It is the git author identity on every commit in a public
// repository, so it is already fetchable from the GitHub API by anybody who
// wants it and hiding it here protects nothing that is not already out. The
// usual mitigations cost more than they save: entity-encoding and "name at
// domain" both break the click for a reader using a mail client and stop no
// scraper written this decade, and a script that assembles the address at
// runtime is script, which this site deliberately does not ship. Spam filtering
// is where that problem is actually handled.
//
// **The state sentence is computed.** How many submissions, by how many people,
// and whether any label has a second claimant are read off the export for the
// same reason they are on the home page and `/about/`: the sentence has to
// change when the corpus does rather than when somebody remembers this file.
//
// **The copy is drafted, not dictated.** Structure and accuracy are mine; the
// sentences are the author's to rewrite.

const EMAIL = "sohampadia10@gmail.com";
const REPO = "https://github.com/controlbun/registry";

const size = data.corpus.size;
const authors = new Set(
  data.labels.flatMap((l) => l.claimants).map((c) => c.author)).size;
const contested = data.model_index.some((m) =>
  m.labels.some((b) => new Set(b.claimants.map((c) => c.author)).size > 1));
---

<Base title="Contact" trail={[{ text: "controlbun", href: "/" }]} synthetic="none">
  <h1 slot="heading">Contact</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Email <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a>. The code is at{" "}
    <a href={REPO}>github.com/controlbun/registry</a>, with the brief, the
    decision log and the tests that hold the pages to what they promise.
  </p>

  <h2>What is worth writing about</h2>

  <p>
    This is {size} submissions by {authors === 1 ? "one author" : `${authors} authors`},
    and what a submission records is a pointer rather than bytes.{!contested && (
      " No label has a second claimant, so the disagreement this is built to " +
      "hold has not happened here once."
    )}
  </p>

  <p>
    The mail that changes that comes from three people: somebody who would
    publish a direction here and can say what would have to be true first,
    somebody who thinks the design is wrong and can say where, and somebody who
    has an artifact and nowhere to put it.
  </p>

  <p class="note">
    Mail is not a submission route: nothing here publishes what you send it, and
    that has not changed. What has changed is that there is now a route, and it
    is <a href="/signed-in/">/signed-in/</a>. Sign in with Hugging Face, fill in
    the pointer and the contract, and press submit. Arriving does not publish it
    either: the bytes are fetched at the pin and checked, the row is written
    where the corpus is rebuilt, and it appears on the site when I publish.
  </p>
</Base>
